#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110395 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "00a8fb88-4b07-57be-94c5-a6b499c9df5b" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f6f648e7-2543-5f0d-8556-9ed0d6ea9f14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3758" :question/problem "Which of the following plots is the graph of $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1486/Source/Images/150b5760d13e7b442290d7eeb5ca5b8f.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n- The domain of the function is $x\\in [-1, 1]$.\n- The range of the function is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$.\n- The minimum value occurs at $x=1$.\n- The maximum value occurs at $x=-1$.\n- The $y$-intercept is $y=\\dfrac \\pi 2$.\n- The function is strictly decreasing on its entire domain.\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is I."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110396 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "039f58e5-0f98-58b1-b2f7-9f0ee33ed075" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "da99c8cf-8609-5a74-b5a0-586912ae88ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3759" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the domain of $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n1. $-2$\n2. $0$\n3. $1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1486/Source/Images/7815bf731f32a207169138c5ead1d527.png)\n\nNote that the domain of the function is\n\n$$\nx=\\left[ -1, 1\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values $x=0$ and $x=1$ lie in the domain of the function, while the value $x=-2$ does not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110397 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "c2e6f348-0412-571d-9b80-0b2bed42a2ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-3760" :question/problem "Which of the following values is in the range of $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n1. $-\\dfrac \\pi 2$\n2. $0$\n3. $\\pi$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1486/Source/Images/b2303b59a361711cf49a0708a4b00b52.png)\n\nNote that the range of the function is\n\n$$\ny=\\left[ 0, \\pi\\right]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values $y=0$ and $y=\\pi$ lie in the range of the function, while the value\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\ndoes not."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110398 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "5dcbabf0-542f-59a7-8ca1-b7461359bd35" :question/math-academy-id "e-3762" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\arccos x$?\n\n1. $\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2,\\dfrac {\\pi}{3}\\right)$\n2. $\\left(0,-\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)$\n3. $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function\n\n$$\ny=\\arccos x\n$$\n\nthe following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\cos x$.\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\arccos x$, which is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi \\right]$.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.\n\n- First, we consider the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3},-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\cos x$. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arccos x$.\n- Next, we consider the point $\\left(0,-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(-\\dfrac\\pi 2,0\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\cos x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$The range of $y=\\arccos x$ is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$, and $y=-\\dfrac\\pi 2$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\quad{\\color{red}{\\times}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(0,-\\dfrac\\pi 2\\right)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\arccos x$.\n- Finally, we consider the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$. We note the following:The point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\cos x.\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ The range of $y=\\arccos x$ is $y\\in \\left[0, \\pi\\right]$, and $y=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ lies within this range. $\\quad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$ Therefore, the point $\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ does lie on the graph of $y=\\arccos x$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b0fd68b-2206-5d60-9ed8-d020751c3e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109824} {:answer/id #uuid "a82e4653-fbbf-5d31-bff0-08f83d38ba2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109825} {:answer/id #uuid "278b7fd9-fea1-53da-adca-6737f493d980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109826} {:answer/id #uuid "510cf5d7-65aa-55ad-8574-1f08d591f1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109827} {:answer/id #uuid "527e8da4-0626-5015-926f-8cdd9dacc30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "527e8da4-0626-5015-926f-8cdd9dacc30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109828} :answer-field/id #uuid "dae94f28-5c2c-538c-9ec1-c2044c4f7886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109823}] :question/id #uuid "4c5ae160-d78c-57fc-8666-ed9ed6778702" :question/math-academy-id "q-65828" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y = \\arccos x$?\n\n1. $(\\frac{π}{3}, \\frac{1}{2})$\n2. $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{7π}{4})$\n3. $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\frac{3π}{4})$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2df217d3-d1d2-54cd-8c93-7925225bfdf3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Range of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8757dece-95aa-566b-8bab-30f826e72b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109842} {:answer/id #uuid "9fbc52a2-0fd3-50c1-93f8-368141fd8aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109843} {:answer/id #uuid "e07d88bf-75db-56fa-b693-7586bc250828" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109844} {:answer/id #uuid "0d12089b-dca3-5bef-9e70-6a728f380b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109845} {:answer/id #uuid "5e1491b2-ec05-5b53-95de-1413fadaf8b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6π}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d12089b-dca3-5bef-9e70-6a728f380b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109845} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e6f917-81bb-52e0-8a92-c59f43e47262" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field